{"seq":14,"ts":"2026-09-23T04:34:36.479Z","kind":"event","event":{"type":"SUBMIT_PHASE","disclosures":[{"choice":"Added subtract(a, b) as a plain function alongside sum and exported both via module.exports = { sum, subtract }","whyItMatters":"The acceptance criteria require subtract to be exported the same way as sum so callers can destructure it exactly like the existing API.","alternatives":[{"option":"Export subtract as a separate/additional export key (e.g. module.exports.sum and module.exports.subtract assigned individually)","consequence":"Would still work but diverge from the existing single-object export style, making the module's surface inconsistent."},{"option":"Define subtract as a one-liner arrow function","consequence":"Stylistically inconsistent with the named function declaration used by sum."}],"recommendation":{"choice":"Use a named function declaration and add it to the shared module.exports object","reason":"Mirrors the existing sum implementation and export style exactly, satisfying the 'exported the same way' criterion with no behavioral surprises."},"classProposal":"detail"},{"choice":"Added one node:test case 'subtract subtracts two numbers' asserting subtract(5, 3) === 2 using the existing assert/strict import","whyItMatters":"Provides a passing node:test test for subtract in the existing style, satisfying the acceptance criteria and keeping the suite green.","alternatives":[{"option":"Test with negative/edge inputs (e.g. subtract(3, 5) === -2)","consequence":"More coverage but introduces behavior not requested and departs from the simple existing test style."},{"option":"Use a nested describe/it structure","consequence":"Would not follow the flat test(...) style of the existing suite."}],"recommendation":{"choice":"Add a single flat test(...) case using assert.equal, matching the existing test","reason":"Follows the established style exactly and is sufficient to prove subtract works as specified."},"classProposal":"detail"}]}}
